WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:02.340
We are addressing the fungal kingdom

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:03.710
and exploring.

00:00:03.710 --> 00:00:06.100
It has profound impacts on

00:00:06.100 --> 00:00:09.440
global human health,
on agriculture and food security,

00:00:09.440 --> 00:00:11.390
and on biodiversity.

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:12.549
So the sorts of questions

00:00:12.549 --> 00:00:14.080
we'd like to get
at is it's very clear

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:15.470
that fungi are the cause

00:00:15.470 --> 00:00:21.360
of devastating threats to food security worldwide,
to human health.

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:25.609
They're killing over 1.5 million people every
year which is more than malaria and they're

00:00:25.609 --> 00:00:31.250
also causing extinctions in real-time of amphibians
for example and bats.

00:00:31.250 --> 00:00:35.410
And so their impact is clear, but we know
very little about where these pathogens have

00:00:35.410 --> 00:00:40.050
originated, how they evolved, these traits
that allow them to cause such devastating

00:00:40.050 --> 00:00:45.170
impact, how they've evolved resistance to
the therapeutics and the agricultural fungicides

00:00:45.170 --> 00:00:47.360
that we use to try to control them.

00:00:47.360 --> 00:00:49.240
So we brought together a consortium

00:00:49.240 --> 00:00:52.000
of individuals
who work on plant fungal pathogens,

00:00:52.000 --> 00:00:53.239
and the

00:00:53.239 --> 00:00:55.830
risk to agriculture and food security,

00:00:55.830 --> 00:00:58.300
individuals
who work on fungal pathogens

00:00:58.300 --> 00:00:59.920
that are decimating

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:02.160
animal populations globally,

00:01:02.160 --> 00:01:06.870
and then investigators
working on human fungal pathogens both physician

00:01:06.870 --> 00:01:13.640
scientists, basic scientists, and bio informatics
genomics experts who are helping reveal key

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.920
features and vulnerabilities of these organisms
that we can exploit for new treatments.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:23.420
So I think the most attractive and enabling
aspect of the CIFAR model for us is that it

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:27.400
allows us to bring together an extraordinarily
inter-disciplinary team.

00:01:27.400 --> 00:01:31.950
So we have assembled leading experts in really
diverse areas that are in sort of traditionally

00:01:31.950 --> 00:01:36.230
disparate disciplines and so we're able to
bring them together under this sort of unique

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:41.560
program to tackle these global grand challenges
that couldn't be accomplished in any other

00:01:41.560 --> 00:01:46.240
means, and we're going to explore sort of
where they came from, how they've evolved

00:01:46.240 --> 00:01:51.360
these traits and leveraged that kind of insights
to develop really new strategies to thwart

00:01:51.360 --> 00:01:53.220
these fungal pathogens.

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:59.460
[Music]

